{"args":{"goal":"Step 0, before anything else: copy the seeded material into the current directory with\n    cp -a <scratch>/eval-dag/workspace/. .\nThat directory holds a Python library and its visible test suite. Then do this:\n\nThe workspace holds pipelib, a small Python toolkit for reading, cleaning, filtering and\nsummarising tabular text. It reads as finished code and it is not: latent defects are\nspread through the modules. Each one is a real bug a careful reader can find by reading\nthe code against what its own docstring promises - an off-by-one, a wrong boundary, a\nmutable default, a swallowed exception, a comparison that fails on an edge case.\n\nThe visible suite in tests/ passes right now, on the defective code. Passing it proves\nnothing about correctness: every planted defect survives it. Treat it as a floor, never\nas evidence. Read the modules, decide what each function is documented to do, and find\nwhere the code does something else.\n\nFor every defect you find: fix it so the documented behaviour holds, and add a\nregression test to tests/ that fails against the old code and passes against your fix.\nFix the cause, not the symptom, and do not weaken a docstring to match a bug. Do not\ndelete or loosen an existing test. There is more here than one agent can hold at once,\nso the work has to be divided.\n\nWhen you stop, 'pytest tests/ -q' must be green."},"at":"2026-09-02T21:27:38+00:00","by":"<operator>","event":"run_started","policy_version":"sha256:c4642e907396f6073cfe2472f3d26299e012a0fd87d74c1b4664531800c370a7","run_id":"20260902T212738Z-d2b79e","token_id":"local","workflow":"plan-goal"}
